---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description } from '../../content';

const path = '/guides/pricing-models';
const title = "Shopify Email App Pricing Models — Operator Guide";
const desc = description(site, 'guides/pricing-models');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Does suppressing a contact reduce the bill?",
    "answer": "Only if the applicable billing definition excludes that state. Confirm the quote and billing behavior before using suppression as a savings assumption."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should I compare annual and monthly plans?",
    "answer": "Use the same workload horizon, show cash due separately from allocated recurring cost, and include commitments, renewal terms and exit costs."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can attributed revenue prove payback?",
    "answer": "Attribution assigns credit under a reporting rule. A cost comparison can show invoice savings; claims of additional profit require separate evidence and a stated measurement method."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="container">
      <a class="link" href="/guides">← All operator guides</a>
      <p class="editorial-kicker mt-6">Pricing Models</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">Turn an email app quote into a usable cost forecast</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">Compare the billable units in the contract with the audience and messages you actually plan to operate. A contact count, subscriber count and send allowance describe different things. This worksheet is for evaluating quotes and renewals; it does not assume a particular supplier is cheapest.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Define the workload before requesting a quote</h2>
      <p>Export stored identities, subscribed addresses, suppressed records, billable profiles as reported by the incumbent, and actual marketing sends for the same billing period. Keep those counts separate. A purchase or an email open is not a substitute for permission, and an engaged audience is not automatically the billed audience. Record which store, channel and account each export covers so combined accounts are not compared with a single-store quote.</p>
      <p>For a replacement quote, ask how imported history, duplicate identities, unsubscribed contacts and suppressed records enter the billable count. Retain the written definition alongside the export. Do not assume suppressing a record lowers the bill or deleting a segment removes the underlying contact.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Normalize currency, period and scope</h2>
      <p>Give every quote a date, currency, billing period, tax treatment, contract term and renewal date. Separate a temporary credit from the recurring price. Record annual commitments as both cash due and cost allocated over the covered months; monthly billing and annual prepayment have different cash consequences. Confirm whether additional stores require separate accounts or fees.</p>
      <p>Build one row per charged unit: base subscription, contacts or subscribers, email sends, SMS usage, seats, integration or analytics add-ons, support and implementation. Note minimum commitments and included allowances. A blank row means unresolved pricing, not zero cost.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Distinguish subscribers, contacts and send volume</h2>
      <p>For a subscriber-priced quote, obtain the exact subscription states and channels counted. For a contact-priced quote, establish whether billing uses a snapshot, maximum during the period or another rule. For a send-priced quote, confirm whether attempted, accepted, delivered, transactional and test messages count. These are questions for the actual quote, not universal platform rules.</p>
      <p>Segment overlap needs its own check. Count unique recipients for an audience estimate, but count each intended message for send usage. A person receiving a campaign and two flow messages contributes multiple sends. Several segments containing the same person do not by themselves establish several billable contacts.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Forecast campaigns and automations separately</h2>
      <p>Start campaigns with eligible recipients multiplied by planned messages, then account for exclusions and overlapping audiences. Model flows from eligible entries and expected reachable steps after purchase exits, permission changes and collision suppression. Keep assumptions visible rather than treating every trigger as a completed sequence. Reconcile the baseline estimate to an actual invoice before projecting growth.</p>
      <p>Prepare a baseline, a capture-growth case and a peak-send case across the chosen planning horizon. Change the variables that create cost: imported identities, signup intake, active journeys, campaign cadence and channel volume. Do not increase all inputs by the same growth percentage unless the operating plan supports it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Apply allowances, tiers and overages to each period</h2>
      <p>Use the quoted pricing schedule to turn each period of workload into a fee. Check tier boundaries, automatic upgrades, overage rates, unused allowance expiry and downgrade timing. Sum the resulting period costs; multiplying the final month by the entire year can misstate a growing workload. A flat average can hide a peak that crosses a tier.</p>
      <p>For SMS, request destination-specific rates, the unit used for message length, carrier or other pass-through fees, credit expiry and whether failed attempts are charged. Keep SMS separate from email allowances. Do not convert a marketing message count into a currency estimate without the quoted unit and rate.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Include the tools that remain in the stack</h2>
      <p>Inventory capture, reviews, loyalty, SMS, data sync, reporting and agency work. Mark each as retained, replaced or unresolved. A bundled feature saves a separate subscription only if it replaces the actual workflow and the old contract can end. Include seats and maintenance work needed to operate any workaround.</p>
      <p>Avoid counting the same labor twice under an agency fee and internal operations. Use incremental operating cost for a switch comparison, while keeping full stack cost visible for budgeting. A cheaper email invoice can coexist with higher total cost if integration support or a capture add-on remains necessary.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Price migration and overlap explicitly</h2>
      <p>List data mapping, permission and suppression transfer, template rebuilds, testing, staff training and archive access. Record hours by owner with a stated costing basis, supplier fees and any overlap subscription. Separate one-time transition cost from recurring operating cost. Use the migration guide for cutover ownership and queue handling.</p>
      <p>Annual savings before transition cost equals incumbent operating cost minus replacement operating cost over the same horizon. Net savings subtract one-time transition cost. If the recurring difference is zero or negative, there is no positive cost-only payback. Do not add speculative recovery revenue to make an otherwise weak savings case appear proven.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Run a quote reconciliation exercise</h2>
      <p>This is a proposed exercise, not an observed merchant result. Give each finalist the same dated workload sheet, audience-state definitions and peak case. Ask for an itemized quote and reproduce it in the worksheet. Where your calculation differs, identify the unit, boundary or allowance responsible before comparing totals.</p>
      <p>Include a tier-edge case and a mid-period import or suppression change. Save supplier responses, unresolved items and the person responsible for resolving them. A quote that cannot be reproduced remains uncertain; it is not evidence that the supplier will bill the lowest interpretation.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Decide on cost and operational fit together</h2>
      <p>Use the forecast to identify which assumptions change the cost ordering. There is no universal engaged-to-total ratio at which a send-priced or contact-priced plan wins. The actual rates, allowances, cadence and required features determine the comparison. Confirm the workflow requirements in the selection framework before treating the lowest total as a viable option.</p>
      <p>Renewal preparation starts from the contractual notice deadline and time needed for a tested alternative. Ask about repricing, export access, cancellation and unused commitments. Set an internal decision date from those dependencies rather than a universal negotiation calendar or a promised discount.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Maintain a finance handoff</h2>
      <p>Deliver the dated quotes, unit definitions, period forecast, scenario totals, migration costs, renewal obligations and unresolved assumptions. Assign an owner to compare invoices with forecasts and explain differences such as capture intake, extra sends or a changed rate. Revisit the model when those drivers change.</p>
      <p>Retain purchase and suppression history according to the applicable data policy rather than deleting records solely to manipulate a quote. Before any cleanup, verify its billing effect and operational consequences. A forecast should make tradeoffs visible without promising savings that depend on untested workflow changes.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><nav aria-label="Related operator guides" class="flex flex-wrap gap-4">
    <a class="link" href="/guides/selection-framework">Selection requirements →</a>
    <a class="link" href="/guides/migration">Migration costs and cutover →</a>
    <a class="link" href="/guides/revenue-attribution">Measurement and profit →</a>
  </nav></section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Pricing models FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
